1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,941
The brush uncovers a small temple — and it has no joints.

2
00:00:03,941 --> 00:00:08,661
It was not built up from blocks; it was carved downward out of a single standing boulder,

3
00:00:08,661 --> 00:00:11,478
like a sculpture that happens to be a building.

4
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,356
The people who made these were working out how a stone temple should look, in the most difficult way imaginable.

5
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:32,511
Inscriptions, including one of the most famous in Indian history.

6
00:00:32,511 --> 00:00:36,505
Excavation and the standing monuments, which are the main evidence.

7
00:00:36,505 --> 00:00:39,742
And texts, which are thinner here than the stone.

8
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,675
Two dynasties, facing each other across the peninsula.

9
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:52,871
The Chalukyas of Badami in the western Deccan, from about 543 CE, with Aihole and Pattadakal nearby.

10
00:00:52,871 --> 00:00:59,430
The Pallavas in the Tamil country, with their capital at Kanchipuram and their port at Mahabalipuram.

11
00:01:02,967 --> 00:01:05,260
They fought each other, with interruptions,

12
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:08,960
for close to two hundred years — capitals were sacked in both directio

13
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,960
ns — and the rivalry is the frame for everything else in this episode.

14
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:24,766
Pulakeshin the Second is the Chalukya ruler to know, and the Aihole inscription, composed by his court poet Ravikirti, is the document.

15
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:35,614
It records his achievements, including checking Harshavardhana at the Narmada — which is how we date Harsha,

16
00:01:35,614 --> 00:01:40,157
and a good illustration of why inscriptions from the other side are so valuable.

17
00:01:40,157 --> 00:01:45,449
He was later defeated, and Badami taken, by the Pallava ruler Narasimhavarman the First.

18
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:56,144
Now the architecture, which is the real reason this period matters.

19
00:01:56,144 --> 00:01:57,431
Watch the sequence.

20
00:01:57,431 --> 00:01:59,749
First, shrines cut into cave faces.

21
00:01:59,749 --> 00:02:07,129
Then monoliths — whole temples carved from single boulders, the rathas at Mahabalipuram being the famous set.

22
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:17,838
Then structural temples, built up from cut blocks, like the Shore Temple at Mahabalipuram and the temples at Pattadakal.

23
00:02:17,838 --> 00:02:23,924
In roughly two centuries the Indian stone temple goes from a hole in a cliff to a free-standing building.

24
00:02:28,233 --> 00:02:30,427
And the two great styles take shape.

25
00:02:30,427 --> 00:02:34,576
In the north, the nagara temple with its curving tower, the shikhara.

26
00:02:34,576 --> 00:02:41,159
In the south, the dravida temple with its stepped pyramidal tower, the vimana, inside a walled enclosure.

27
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:49,795
Pattadakal is the place to remember, because temples in both styles stand there together,

28
00:02:49,795 --> 00:02:53,645
which is why historians read it as a site where the traditions met.

29
00:02:53,645 --> 00:02:57,003
A third term, vesara, is used for mixed Deccan forms.

30
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:08,318
Mahabalipuram is also called Mamallapuram, from Mamalla, a title of Narasimhavarman the First.

31
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,037
And a caution about the rathas: they are named after the Pandavas and Draupadi,

32
00:03:16,037 --> 00:03:21,536
but those names are a later convention and do not tell you anything about what the monuments were for.

33
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:29,046
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:29,046 --> 00:03:33,501
Four places: Aihole, Badami, Pattadakal — Chalukya.

35
00:03:33,501 --> 00:03:35,943
Mahabalipuram — Pallava.

36
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:42,353
Dig Quiz.

37
00:03:42,353 --> 00:03:43,657
Pause and name it.

38
00:03:43,657 --> 00:03:51,715
First: the inscription composed by Ravikirti that records Pulakeshin the Second's achievements and dates Harsha.

39
00:03:56,833 --> 00:03:58,351
The Aihole inscription.

40
00:03:58,351 --> 00:04:03,092
Second: the northern temple style, with a curving tower called a shikhara.

41
00:04:03,092 --> 00:04:03,897
Nagara.

42
00:04:07,433 --> 00:04:13,123
Third: the southern style, with a stepped pyramidal vimana inside a walled enclosure.

43
00:04:13,123 --> 00:04:13,719
Dravida.

44
00:04:13,719 --> 00:04:19,113
In Mains, a frequent question asks how Indian temple architecture developed.

45
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:31,847
Give the sequence — cave, monolith, structural — and then the two styles, with Pattadakal as the place where both appear.

46
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:41,393
A monolithic ratha takes its place in the gallery, a building carved like a sculpture.

47
00:04:45,533 --> 00:04:50,909
Next gallery: three powers, one city, and two hundred years of fighting over it.

48
00:04:50,909 --> 00:04:54,991
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

